All,

You will find in the Philmac Valve price sheet the following configurations=
:


=B7         UTC B x 1" FNPT, Ball Valve w/Tee Handle

=B7         UTC C x 1" FNPT

=B7         UTC B x UTC B

=B7         UTC C x UTC C

These valves are targeted at (but not limited to) repair and replacement ap=
plications in water service lines.

In the case of the version with an FNPT outlet the size of this threaded ou=
tlet is not relevant when this valve is used as a "Capping Valve".  In the =
capping scenario the FNPT end is not used to connect to anything (not that =
it couldn't be).  The valve is used to cap off a line that is either UTC "B=
" size or UTC "C" size while it is being repaired.  The final connection is=
 made later by other means.

The reducing version of UTC adapters allows us to use one size valve (in th=
is case 1") to serve two sizes of lines, UTC B and UTC C.  We make these va=
lves by assembling MNPT x UTC Adapters into FNPT x FNPT Valves.

See the UTC literature for the pipe and tubing types and sizes that UTC B a=
nd UTC C can connect.

In broad stokes:  but not limited to...

=B7         UTC B fits =BE" Copper Pipe/Tubing and Copper Tube Size PE Pipe=
/Tubing (CTS PE)

=B7         UTC C fits 1"

If one were looking at the price sheet for a "capping valve" for =BE" lines=
 one might erroneously conclude there was no valve for =BE" because your ey=
e and mind are registering the 1" FNPT and not the UTC B or UTC C inlet.  I=
n fact the UTC B x 1" valve would be available and the appropriate selectio=
n for =BE" Copper or CTS PE tubing.

Our literature might be refined but in the meantime this is your guidance.

j



